  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nRain cleared the CWA just before noon today with breezy southerly \nand southeasterly winds gusting 25 to 30 MPH. Most places ended up \nwith 0.20 to 0.40\" with some embedded higher totals greater than a \nhalf inch. There are some breaks in the clouds, but overall \nexpecting mostly cloudy skies which will help keep our temperatures \nin check. We won't be as lucky tomorrow with continued southerly \nflow aloft sending us ample warm, moist air. Apparent Ts may reach \nthe mid to upper 90s across southern and western Minnesota with \nupper 80s to low 90s elsewhere. Precip chances have decreased for \nmuch of the daytime hours tomorrow. A subtle wave overnight may lead \nto some isolated showers and a rumble of thunder early Sunday for \nfar western Minnesota. The better chance for showers and storms will \narrive after sunset across western and central Minnesota into \nwestern Wisconsin as a low moves out of the Dakotas. This system \nwill bring a cold front through the region, but the most it will do \nin terms of sensible weather impacts is lower Tds for Tuesday. This \nFROPA Monday afternoon will once again lead to some isolated showers \nalong the boundary, mainly in western Wisconsin. \n\nPoPs increase Tuesday evening with another shortwave moving out of \nthe Dakotas. This will likely be another widespread light rainfall \nevent like we saw early this morning with QPF amounts around 0.20\" \nwith localized higher totals. With the upper ridge shifting into the \neastern CONUS, we will find ourselves with nearly daily chances of \nshowers with embedded waves parading through. Temperatures will \ncontinue to be above normal in the low to mid 80s through the end of \nthe period with PWATs running some 150 percent of normal. Those who \nwere hoping September would bring Fall-like weather will have to \nwait.",
